"L" <L_1_L_0@yahoo.com> writes:
> I once heard someone say that if one takes a Sokoban puzzles and remove
> an arbitrary selection of the "boulders" (as well as their
> corresponding goal squares) from the board, the resulting puzzle
> remains "beatable."

Provably beatable (provided the initial puzzle was) and also easier
than the original.

However, consider the following YANI:

Take a sokoban map with all boxes^H^H boulders on their solution squares.
Now, imagine an agent able to *pull* single boulders in orthogonal
directions (basically, the opposite of what Soko players can do).
Turn this agent loose and let him pseudo-randomly drag boulders around
for a bounded number of moves. Put the downstairs on the location of
the agent when this movement ended.

Presto, a sorta-random guaranteed-solvable sokoban level.

Of course, the devil's in little details like: How do you come up
with interesting map outlines (choose from a limited set)? How does
the agent decide which boulder to drag next, and where?

My suspicion is that you'd tend to end up with levels which were
much easier than those hand-crafted by humans. But at least it would
no longer be a "go to spoiler, perform listed moves in order" affair.

Mimics could be used to good effect to even out the difficulty a bit.


